The issue here is that `fmodl`/`fmodf128` is not yet provided by `compiler-builtins` (as it's part of `libm`), and not all platforms provide such a symbol in their system libraries. I suggest instead splitting the test such that the fmod operator (`%`) is only tested when `cfg(reliable_f128_math)` is set whereas the other math operators retain their greater test coverage.

```suggestion
#[test]
fn test_num_f128() {
    // FIXME(f16_f128): replace with a `test_num` call once the required `fmodl`/`fmodf128` function is available on all platforms.
    let ten = 10f128;
    let two = 2f128;
    assert_eq!(ten.add(two), ten + two);
    assert_eq!(ten.sub(two), ten - two);
    assert_eq!(ten.mul(two), ten * two);
    assert_eq!(ten.div(two), ten / two);
}

#[test]
#[cfg(reliable_f128_math)]
fn test_num_f128_rem() {
    let ten = 10f128;
    let two = 2f128;
    assert_eq!(ten.rem(two), ten % two);
}
```

