I've started to experiment with the impl a bit without having reviewed the code yet.
I've found out that these changes lead to an ICE for the following input:

```rs
pub trait A { type T; type U; }

impl A for () {
    type T = Self::U;
    type U = ();
}
```

```
error: internal compiler error: src/librustdoc/html/render/span_map.rs:224:39: no `hir_enclosing_body_owner` for hir_id `HirId(DefId(0:7 ~ check[de40]::{impl#0}::T).1)`
```

That's because not everything has a body (owner), only functions and constants do but every other context doesn't like here: `Self::U` is "inside" assoc type `T` which in turn is inside an `impl`, both aren't body owners since the RHS of `type T` is not a `Body`.

Also ICEs for the same reason:

```rs
pub trait A { type X; }

pub struct F<T: A>(pub T::X);
```

